1
00:00:01,010 --> 00:00:03,840
Let's begin today,
today's morning session.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:05,840
So we're going to go over
differential equations

3
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:09,260
and plotting in Julia.

4
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:11,540
Both of these are two
concepts that are basically

5
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:13,980
the backbone of Neuroblox.

6
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:17,930
And in particular, we're going
to talk about modeling toolkits

7
00:00:17,930 --> 00:00:22,331
and the Makie ecosystem.

8
00:00:22,331 --> 00:00:24,290
And first things first.

9
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:27,380
Modeling toolkit is
a modeling language

10
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:31,820
within the scientific machine
learning ecosystem in Julia,

11
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:35,810
where you can write down systems
of differential equations

12
00:00:35,810 --> 00:00:37,080
in a symbolic manner.

13
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:38,880
It's a very intuitive
way of running them.

14
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,310
I showed you an example
of that yesterday

15
00:00:41,310 --> 00:00:44,000
when I was talking about
how expressive Julia can

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,610
be in its syntax.

17
00:00:46,610 --> 00:00:50,250
And then we'll go over Makie.

18
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:52,188
So Makie is another ecosystem.

19
00:00:52,188 --> 00:00:54,230
And by ecosystem, I mean
essentially a collection

20
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:57,590
of packages that
work with each other

21
00:00:57,590 --> 00:01:02,740
and share some common utilities.

22
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:05,740
And Makie is one
of the alternatives

23
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:10,220
you have if you want to plot
anything, any data in Julia.

24
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:12,640
And this is our
go-to in Neuroblox

25
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:14,920
and I will show you why.

26
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:19,690
And also, yeah, these two
things, modeling toolkit

27
00:01:19,690 --> 00:01:21,520
and Makie go well
together, and you will

28
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:24,910
see that in the examples too.

29
00:01:24,910 --> 00:01:28,790
So, starting with the modeling
toolkit, as I mentioned,

30
00:01:28,790 --> 00:01:31,940
it is a part of the scientific
machine learning ecosystem.

31
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:35,350
And here, I just want to
show you a graph, basically,

32
00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:38,080
of the SCIMLE ecosystem
because it's consisting

33
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:39,650
of a lot of packages.

34
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:41,410
There is a lot going on here.

35
00:01:41,410 --> 00:01:44,960
You can see modeling
toolkit on the right there.

36
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:48,430
And there is even the
differential equations

37
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:50,950
where it's a more programmatic
way of writing differential

38
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:53,890
equations without the
nice symbolic syntax.

39
00:01:53,890 --> 00:01:57,280
There are packages to do
partial differential equations.

40
00:01:57,280 --> 00:01:58,930
These have to be
specialized because

41
00:01:58,930 --> 00:02:00,960
of the nature of those systems.

42
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:05,470
Uncertainty quantification
on your [INAUDIBLE] system,

43
00:02:05,470 --> 00:02:08,039
and there is even
some new utilities

44
00:02:08,039 --> 00:02:10,020
like universal
differential equations

45
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:13,170
and attaching a neural
network inside your equations

46
00:02:13,170 --> 00:02:16,110
to discover missing terms.

47
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:18,150
But out of all
this ecosystem, we

48
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:20,550
will focus on modeling toolkit
because this will allow us

49
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:22,360
to design new blocks as well.

50
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:24,930
And this is how blocks
in Neuroblox blocks

51
00:02:24,930 --> 00:02:28,140
currently are designed.

52
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:32,550
So this is an example from
the modeling toolkit--

53
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:35,040
actually, a course
on modeling toolkit

54
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:39,420
that actually highlights its
modular design and Neuroblox

55
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,530
shares this value very much.

56
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:43,740
So here, we have
essentially one designed

57
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:48,030
system that is a simple
mass and a damper,

58
00:02:48,030 --> 00:02:50,910
a simple mechanical system,
and let it oscillate.

59
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:55,630
So first, we design the
mass as a system on its own.

60
00:02:55,630 --> 00:03:00,950
We write down its
variables and its equations

61
00:03:00,950 --> 00:03:04,110
and other component to
it, which is a port.

62
00:03:04,110 --> 00:03:06,420
And this port will
connect to the damper.

63
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:09,808
So moving on to the
damper definition,

64
00:03:09,808 --> 00:03:11,850
likewise, we have the
variables and the equations

65
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:13,560
in another port.

66
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:16,730
And up there on the right,
you can see the definition

67
00:03:16,730 --> 00:03:18,300
of a port, what that is.

68
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:22,400
And eventually, we want to
define another model that

69
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:24,710
is our system that brings
together our previous two

70
00:03:24,710 --> 00:03:25,560
models.

71
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:29,870
So you can see modeling toolkit
can be very, very modular

72
00:03:29,870 --> 00:03:32,760
and very flexible in its design.

73
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,040
And something similar is
going on in Neuroblox.

74
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:36,980
We have separate
components, and we

75
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:41,000
can link them together
to create a new component

76
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,080
like that system is, right?

77
00:03:43,080 --> 00:03:47,330
If you notice that all of these
components have this [INAUDIBLE]

78
00:03:47,330 --> 00:03:49,590
model macro, right.

79
00:03:49,590 --> 00:03:51,890
So they're all different models.

80
00:03:51,890 --> 00:03:54,420
But some of them,
like the final system,

81
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:55,920
contain other models in them.

82
00:03:55,920 --> 00:03:58,400
So, similar to how
in Neuroblox, we can

83
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:01,210
have some boxes that contain
other boxes in them to have

84
00:04:01,210 --> 00:04:05,560
this multi-scale approach.

85
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:10,450
And also, this SCIMLE ecosystem
comes with an extended solvers

86
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:11,530
library.

87
00:04:11,530 --> 00:04:15,190
Here, you can see some
examples from the documentation

88
00:04:15,190 --> 00:04:21,260
of some of the
[INAUDIBLE] docs in SCIMLE

89
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:23,770
where basically,
these are matches

90
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:26,410
between some popular
solvers that people

91
00:04:26,410 --> 00:04:30,280
use in MATLAB and Python
or R and the equivalent

92
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:31,610
solver in Julia.

93
00:04:31,610 --> 00:04:33,290
So this helps
you-- for instance,

94
00:04:33,290 --> 00:04:38,620
if you already have your model
of differential equations

95
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:42,490
written in another language,
it's easy to port it in Julia

96
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:44,710
and actually match your
previous results to make sure

97
00:04:44,710 --> 00:04:47,530
that the porting was
right because we have

98
00:04:47,530 --> 00:04:48,790
all these solvers available.

99
00:04:48,790 --> 00:04:51,400
And in fact, we even have
more solvers available.

100
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:55,840
Helmut touched on this subject
as well, and on the same page

101
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:58,177
that I link up
there in the docs,

102
00:04:58,177 --> 00:04:59,760
this is actually a
very extensive page

103
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:01,802
that gives you recommendations
about which solver

104
00:05:01,802 --> 00:05:03,750
to use at what situation.

105
00:05:03,750 --> 00:05:06,390
It goes over stiffness,
different levels

106
00:05:06,390 --> 00:05:10,888
of stiffness, the tolerances
that you're asking for--

107
00:05:10,888 --> 00:05:11,680
everything, really.

108
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:13,140
It's very extensive.

109
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:17,490
So, yeah, the solvers are very
well supported and documented

110
00:05:17,490 --> 00:05:19,500
in SCIMLE.

111
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:24,030
And also, again, this is a
reminder of the performance

112
00:05:24,030 --> 00:05:25,680
that the solvers can exhibit.

113
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:28,000
Helmut also show you
a slide like this.

114
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,460
Here, we have two systems.

115
00:05:30,460 --> 00:05:33,810
The Lotka-Volterra system
on top is a simple two

116
00:05:33,810 --> 00:05:35,680
dimensional system
that is not stiff,

117
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:39,610
and the higher system is
eight differential equations.

118
00:05:39,610 --> 00:05:43,390
But it also has a stiff nature.

119
00:05:43,390 --> 00:05:46,890
So if you notice more
closely, the solvers

120
00:05:46,890 --> 00:05:48,910
that we use in each
case are different.

121
00:05:48,910 --> 00:05:50,460
But the color
coding is the same,

122
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:52,420
and it's split
between languages.

123
00:05:52,420 --> 00:05:55,290
And you can see that
in both of these cases,

124
00:05:55,290 --> 00:05:59,070
[INAUDIBLE] is the fastest
for any given error level,

125
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:05,790
especially when we reduce
the error by a lot in--

126
00:06:05,790 --> 00:06:07,470
yeah, in both of these cases.

127
00:06:07,470 --> 00:06:11,240
So our solvers can
be very performant

128
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:13,760
compared to other languages.

129
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:17,600
And also today, I touched on
the subject of emerging features

130
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:20,510
in Julia, where you can
have packages that have not

131
00:06:20,510 --> 00:06:22,170
been designed together.

132
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:24,030
They're not even part
of the same ecosystem.

133
00:06:24,030 --> 00:06:26,720
But it just so happens
because of Julia's design

134
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:29,180
that they can actually
collaborate with each other

135
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:30,870
with minimal effort.

136
00:06:30,870 --> 00:06:34,430
So this is a story like
this with modeling toolkit

137
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:35,460
and measurements.

138
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:38,790
So modeling toolkit is part
of the SCIMLE ecosystem,

139
00:06:38,790 --> 00:06:42,060
and yeah, we've been through it.

140
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:46,050
It's a package to define
differential equation systems.

141
00:06:46,050 --> 00:06:48,500
But measurement
[INAUDIBLE], that's

142
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:51,030
a package in the Julia
physics ecosystem.

143
00:06:51,030 --> 00:06:53,120
So, completely separate
people developing it

144
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:54,780
in completely
separate ecosystems.

145
00:06:54,780 --> 00:07:00,892
And measurements is a package
to do uncertainty quantification

146
00:07:00,892 --> 00:07:01,600
and measurements.

147
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:05,130
So all these plus
minus signs, these two

148
00:07:05,130 --> 00:07:07,410
on when we set the
parameter or when

149
00:07:07,410 --> 00:07:10,710
we set an initial
condition here,

150
00:07:10,710 --> 00:07:12,610
these come from
measurements, right?

151
00:07:12,610 --> 00:07:15,720
But now we add them
to variables that

152
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:19,200
are relevant to modeling
toolkits, and it just works.

153
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,270
There was really a
minimal extra effort

154
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:23,710
that took place in order
to make this work, right?

155
00:07:23,710 --> 00:07:25,990
So we can bring
these two together.

156
00:07:25,990 --> 00:07:27,780
And on the right
here, I show you

157
00:07:27,780 --> 00:07:31,230
a result of essentially
these two packages

158
00:07:31,230 --> 00:07:34,710
collaborating where when we
simulate this system, which

159
00:07:34,710 --> 00:07:38,130
is a simple exponential decay,
if we were to simulate it

160
00:07:38,130 --> 00:07:42,750
without the measurement
uncertainty quantification, then

161
00:07:42,750 --> 00:07:44,620
you would only get a
single line, right?

162
00:07:44,620 --> 00:07:45,910
A single exponential decay.

163
00:07:45,910 --> 00:07:48,030
But because you add
these plus minus signs

164
00:07:48,030 --> 00:07:51,010
in both the parameter value
and the initial condition,

165
00:07:51,010 --> 00:07:54,120
then you have these bounds
around your solution

166
00:07:54,120 --> 00:07:58,100
that is essentially the same
uncertainty propagated forward

167
00:07:58,100 --> 00:08:00,010
to the integrator and
to the final solution.

168
00:08:03,470 --> 00:08:06,210
Moving on to Makie,
so as I said,

169
00:08:06,210 --> 00:08:09,660
Makie is an ecosystem in
itself about plotting,

170
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:14,100
and it comes with four
different backends.

171
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:17,190
So Makie is there
as an interface.

172
00:08:17,190 --> 00:08:19,860
It sets all the
plotting functions.

173
00:08:19,860 --> 00:08:23,498
But the actual rendering is
happening on the backend, right?

174
00:08:23,498 --> 00:08:25,290
And there are four
options for the backend.

175
00:08:25,290 --> 00:08:28,050
There is [INAUDIBLE], which is
the one that we're going to use

176
00:08:28,050 --> 00:08:32,760
and the one that Neuroblox is
using internally for 2D graphics

177
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:36,530
that can be the publication
quality graphics.

178
00:08:36,530 --> 00:08:40,380
There's glmakie for GPU
power 2D and 3D plots,

179
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:43,429
so if you have something
computationally very heavy

180
00:08:43,429 --> 00:08:44,250
to plot.

181
00:08:44,250 --> 00:08:49,160
And you can exploit your GPU
and use glmakie with this.

182
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:54,080
wglmakie, which is a web-based,
and it's also interactive both

183
00:08:54,080 --> 00:08:58,090
in 2D and 3D, and there is an
experimental package that is

184
00:08:58,090 --> 00:09:02,420
being developed now, that
rprmakie that does ray tracing,

185
00:09:02,420 --> 00:09:06,430
so for more photorealistic
graphics and I also link

186
00:09:06,430 --> 00:09:11,350
beautiful.makie.org there
because I think it's a very good

187
00:09:11,350 --> 00:09:13,340
example of what Makie can do.

188
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:17,110
So if you want to convince
yourself to use Makie,

189
00:09:17,110 --> 00:09:20,170
then go check that page
and see what actually

190
00:09:20,170 --> 00:09:25,000
one person that runs it, what
he is able to make in Makie.

191
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,550
So you can see what's possible.

192
00:09:27,550 --> 00:09:30,280
But also within
the Mac ecosystem,

193
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:34,960
there's the extended ecosystem
besides the core functionality

194
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:39,740
where people have used Makie to
make different kinds of plots.

195
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:41,980
And of course, these are
all open source and part

196
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:44,930
of the extended ecosystem.

197
00:09:44,930 --> 00:09:48,290
So, unfold Makie, very
useful for neuroscience.

198
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:50,470
There is a package
to-- well, that

199
00:09:50,470 --> 00:09:52,330
does all these plots
that you can see here

200
00:09:52,330 --> 00:09:54,850
from power spectrums.

201
00:09:54,850 --> 00:09:57,390
Even power spectrums
map on the scalp

202
00:09:57,390 --> 00:10:01,470
that you could use as AED
and source localization

203
00:10:01,470 --> 00:10:04,900
and event-related potentials.

204
00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:06,390
And then there's
also pair plots,

205
00:10:06,390 --> 00:10:09,240
where you can do
essentially, this triangular

206
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:13,980
plot of combinations of
different correlations

207
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:19,000
between your parameters,
geomakie geographical plots,

208
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:20,850
and there's also
multibody, which

209
00:10:20,850 --> 00:10:22,810
is about multibody physics.

210
00:10:22,810 --> 00:10:26,160
And you can actually
design robotic arms

211
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:30,450
like that or any
body out of boxes

212
00:10:30,450 --> 00:10:32,190
and have it animate as well.

213
00:10:32,190 --> 00:10:33,760
And this is only a subset.

214
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:36,210
If you go to the
Makie website, you

215
00:10:36,210 --> 00:10:39,990
will see that this extended
ecosystem is quite large.

216
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:45,570
So there are way
more examples in it.

217
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:48,900
And finally, I want to
touch on Makie layouts

218
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:52,140
because I think this is one of
the main features that makes

219
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:55,540
Makie attractive to new users.

220
00:10:55,540 --> 00:10:58,010
It certainly did it for me.

221
00:10:58,010 --> 00:11:00,510
You will see it in
today's exercise as well.

222
00:11:00,510 --> 00:11:02,660
It's very easy to
make layouts like this

223
00:11:02,660 --> 00:11:04,640
when you have subplots.

224
00:11:04,640 --> 00:11:08,370
And maybe some subplots are
split into more subplots.

225
00:11:08,370 --> 00:11:10,500
For instance, panel A
here, as you can see,

226
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:13,200
there are three plots
there and a legend.

227
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:16,460
And it's very easy to organize
them in a single square,

228
00:11:16,460 --> 00:11:20,900
right, next to C, which is just
an image of a brain and then

229
00:11:20,900 --> 00:11:24,530
the other two plots, again,
with multiple subplots.

230
00:11:24,530 --> 00:11:27,650
And if you want to follow
this layout example,

231
00:11:27,650 --> 00:11:34,370
you can do this on the Makie
website on the layout tutorials,

232
00:11:34,370 --> 00:11:35,100
basically.

233
00:11:35,100 --> 00:11:38,480
And if you're coming
from, for instance, R,

234
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:42,000
and you know ggplot2, which
is a different syntax--

235
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:43,880
it's using this grammar
of graphics syntax

236
00:11:43,880 --> 00:11:45,000
for designing graphics.

237
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,140
This is also possible with
Makie by using this algebra

238
00:11:48,140 --> 00:11:50,630
of graphics package.

239
00:11:50,630 --> 00:11:53,910
And also, yeah, I would say that
if you're coming from Python,

240
00:11:53,910 --> 00:11:59,300
the syntax in designing plots
looks more like matplotlib

241
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:05,050
if you were to use base Makie
without the algebra of graphics.

242
00:12:05,050 --> 00:12:06,770
So yeah, this is all for now.

243
00:12:06,770 --> 00:12:12,460
And in terms of notebooks, you
can navigate on the website

244
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:13,010
as well.

245
00:12:13,010 --> 00:12:16,040
We'll cover the differential
equations in Julia section

246
00:12:16,040 --> 00:12:17,540
and the plotting
with Makie section.

247
00:12:17,540 --> 00:12:20,560
So please start with
differential equations in Julia.

248
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:22,990
Again, you can see
exactly the same material

249
00:12:22,990 --> 00:12:25,602
in notebook or the website.

250
